#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147310 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d058486e-3e14-5649-815a-7730311c5aec" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For an Exponential Growth or Decay Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "f47ee36e-c497-5f63-9e2b-b873af5600e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6671" :question/problem "Let $y = \\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}$ be a series solution to the equation $y'-3y=0$.\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients $a_n$, where $n=0,1,2, \\ldots$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $y'(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y'(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty n a_n x^{n-1}-3\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n = 0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-1$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+1) a_{m+1} x^{m} -3 \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n =0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty (n+1) a_{n+1} x^{n} -3 \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n =0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0$, we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-3{a}_{n}{x}^{n}] & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}-3{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n(n+1) a_{n+1}  -3 a_n = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} =\\dfrac{3 a_n }{n+1}, \\qquad n= 0,1,2, \\ldots\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147311 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1980ddeb-37bf-5234-8888-f8292e2c9405" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For a Second-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "5466a51e-7dc5-55e6-a269-869cc7b94e83" :question/math-academy-id "e-6672" :question/problem "Let $y =\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}$ be a series solution to the equation $2y''+y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for $a_n$ can be written as\n\n$$\na_{n+2} = \\dfrac{ a_n }{ f(n) }, \\qquad n= 0,1,2\\ldots\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $y'(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find $y''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y''(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty n (n-1) a_{n} x^{n-2} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+2) (m+1) a_{m+2} x^{m} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty (n+2) (n+1) a_{n+2} x^{n} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[2(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}+{a}_{n}{x}^{n}] & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[2(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}+{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n2(n+2)(n+1) a_{n+2}  +a_n = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+2} = -\\dfrac{ a_n }{ 2(n+1)(n+2)  }, \\qquad n= 0,1,2 \\ldots\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(n)=-2(n+1)(n+2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147312 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ab3f10-aca4-5094-98d2-0de9c11fd711" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "d8617bae-7f0f-50d3-8b1a-23b7622956b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6673" :question/problem "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/2ad87a5ee9c27f6d3f03b098b804613d81bc91bb5a811b7655e12efcb052f1e8.png) be a series solution to the equation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/5379e686b1ac80c3cb5fec6c41ae2e281b7db590044ff70ff9c3068ac1332f98.png)\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/527b41b4ced63af6b53983c8aee27c21a7651f52abd01b7600d4e2a1102db63f.png) where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/6ce3483e55b55dee0411597e321facef4f770900e379d1f82da2ceaf278ef40c.png)?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/29dbde1998029c25aedffc9dccb1bbaf47781dc5816a69da0256952d82009ef0.png)\n![](/home