ake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/c07274e7bcff0fa7fb2912ede95bbc9ba7c31af65b3e4fc0613174ca843bccc4.png) with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/56de185c0e7e838b89aec2ec1cee1ebc90029112047f9cebc42de13a1bcf3f95.png) in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/fe5b5c122194b65330b9c7bbd1530ded53b90f4bdd98d79dd5794942e08599a5.png)\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/412e3e48aabdabf0dbdfce8e5b8e6e24c7558d50640bfb31ad4b642d9de39ff3.png) So, we take out the term ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/63890006ecc80706f3bce27717772762e7fb252410047a230519f4d926b5b988.png) from the left-hand side:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/9111081928de5b15aeca089185c43c60a70d9ca2f0eb96c9872ab04204b16297.png)\n\nWe now combine the series as follows:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/bc7ab05dbf97481578fcc8352d76608b3cfe2b7b4301af51eadd810c160a247b.png)\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/977bbc80efee8cdaf6667c4ced8e836d3fb77c53e232065541b1ed72594cada3.png) to zero, we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/04082d8431a1c86db2282e85065a089bd3cb18082ae278d03831bcde599e56f0.png)\n\nSolving for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/fc1f5e662d05224ca25f4893c62389cc5d8b11acbae73f269a7921e097374b85.png) gives\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/683610152cf39c63fcf62ccdeadf1beed4ff8ef1cfb6a28452420974901cdd0c.png)\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/7ce940ebe652683318187b453d18127a4334b5c0dfef68da5e9a74e937f2d734.png)\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index equals ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/0021ff77f0c4565f235da704e0b4d45d1145486e46d721ea834cba941061b020.png) since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/ea7eeb2aa3909170b1ed83f408f8b17ec17d5012db54fd49910e85034899dbb8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d058486e-3e14-5649-815a-7730311c5aec" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For an Exponential Growth or Decay Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f19f8ec1-bc62-5f59-af3c-85bc4d90ba36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-12{a}_{n}(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316664} {:answer/id #uuid "c853958f-241f-55ad-aa67-e095d8e08b70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{4{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316665} {:answer/id #uuid "85021ec1-3345-5816-878e-b8f7bc3b2ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=4{a}_{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316666} {:answer/id #uuid "3af091e8-0110-5873-8ba2-360fa711cd20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{12{a}_{n}}{(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316667} {:answer/id #uuid "1501873c-d3d3-552f-af5a-163a4af170e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{4{a}_{n}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c853958f-241f-55ad-aa67-e095d8e08b70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{4{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316665} :answer-field/id #uuid "29e0027d-ad5b-5d1b-b87c-5afa4dd7fa26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac34c253-b1a1-583e-93d2-70299030c461" :question/math-academy-id "q-101381" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{′}-4y=0.$ \n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=0,1,2,…?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[3(n+1){a}_{n+1}-4{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n3(n+1){a}_{n+1}-4{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{4{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)},\\,n=0,1,2,….\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1980ddeb-37bf-5234-8888-f8292e2c9405" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For a Second-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c5d0022-e547-5ed1-b625-6399cec56f9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{3(n+1)(n+2)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316671} {:answer/id #uuid "31d2b2b9-0b14-510a-8b86-ebad695dcb37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{3n(n+1)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316672} {:answer/id #uuid "77c5420c-2488-5f62-9deb-2060ca669c4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316673} {:answer/id #uuid "c90b606c-5d71-532f-a2f4-29c32b715a6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{2}{3(n+1)(n+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316674} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5c3009-85a9-5ff5-a0f2-371c2b3c9461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{2}{3n(n+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c5d0022-e547-5ed1-b625-6399cec56f9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{3(n+1)(n+2)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316671} :answer-field/id #uuid "63e2365f-25a8-5496-bf56-3065f105ff0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df28548c-91d7-53ca-be69-ef2a161ac056" :question/math-academy-id "q-101385" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{″}-2y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}-2{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}-2{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}-2{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[3(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-2{a}_{n}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n3(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-2{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{2{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=\\frac{3(n+1)(n+2)}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ab3f10-aca4-5094-98d2-0de9c11fd711" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62f962da-53c8-5127-8f00-512a091dfd2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316678} {:answer/id #uuid "5e2335c9-6bf5-56d4-b4cb-65880c59866a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-2{a}_{n}(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316679} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce5b765-6e95-5b58-a615-7d8f2d0beb92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{2n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316680} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb0cc05-e490-5139-9c90-9fd43de5461f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-{a}_{n-1}(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316681} {:answer/id #uuid "77b01364-5fc3-5673-8848-4dea5c0bb829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77b01364-5fc3-5673-8848-4dea5c0bb829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316682} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f7a4ef0-2bcc-5936-b05d-5797ef983519" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c4b94f8d-a475-55bb-bec7-595ae4adc188" :question/math-academy-id "q-101402" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $6{y}^{′}+3xy=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+3x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }6(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }6(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }6(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n6{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n6{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(n+1){a}_{n+1}+3{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,6(n+1){a}_{n+1}=-3{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2(n+1)},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d058486e-3e14-5649-815a-7730311c5aec" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For an Exponential Growth or Decay Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f1ddf92-3419-5130-9a50-5a478e600b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{12(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316685} {:answer/id #uuid "d6070e63-a296-5ced-8a35-479e789d1069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{(4n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316686} {:answer/id #uuid "d01b0f44-cfa0-5791-b7c5-1ff0a154c9bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=12{a}_{n}(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316687} {:answer/id #uuid "b2f1bf81-cb4f-54ed-bb5f-06086f7c6d84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3{a}_{n}}{(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316688} {:answer/id #uuid "049cf688-ebd9-58c9-8635-b3f7e0af4380" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "049cf688-ebd9-58c9-8635-b3f7e0af4380" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316689} :answer-field/id #uuid "144be257-7345-54c9-a5a8-1cdd34e8c412" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff5a4452-7358-5569-954f-338c9c8a6546" :question/math-academy-id "q-102304" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $4y+12{y}^{′}=0.$ \n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=0,1,2,…?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+12{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the second sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+12{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the second summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+12{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[4{a}_{n}+12(n+1){a}_{n+1}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n4{a}_{n}+12(n+1){a}_{n+1}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)},\\,n=0,1,2,….\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1980ddeb-37bf-5234-8888-f8292e2c9405" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For a Second-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d6ef4d2-c301-5294-a992-c5c6687434d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=-(n+1)(n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316692} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3cd709-33c6-5cca-8e51-327038495ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=(n+1)(n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316693} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb241d9-48b8-56d7-bce5-4b82a37d692d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{1}{(n+1)(n+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316694} {:answer/id #uuid "2676b077-1d2e-5511-b74c-cc2b2464f8e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=-(2n+1)(n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316695} {:answer/id #uuid "ac45d126-6a9c-58d3-a64f-0c9409bb3c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=-\\frac{1}{(n+1)(n+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d6ef4d2-c301-5294-a992-c5c6687434d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=-(n+1)(n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316692} :answer-field/id #uuid "ced13391-0ed6-5f15-aa4d-b3705637054e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26bebd5f-bd47-51cf-9d35-830618cac2ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-102307" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $y+{y}^{″}=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the second sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the second summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[{a}_{n}+(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}+(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=-(n+1)(n+2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1980ddeb-37bf-5234-8888-f8292e2c9405" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For a Second-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56226db1-0b12-5d9d-9ac6-51269caeaffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=-6(n+1)(n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316699} {:answer/id #uuid "d63470cd-c0e5-5bbb-b15a-4c275349c0e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=-\\frac{2(n+1)(n+2)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316700} {:answer/id #uuid "9d24b7b6-7fb4-525d-a76f-a6d79a1b7b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=-(2n+1)(3n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316701} {:answer/id #uuid "0f43337a-c15a-57f5-a55f-0ceb4656a2bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=-\\frac{3(n+1)(n+2)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316702} {:answer/id #uuid "266767e3-5192-5cf1-b5b4-6792ee0223d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=-\\frac{2}{3(n+1)(n+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f43337a-c15a-57f5-a55f-0ceb4656a2bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=-\\frac{3(n+1)(n+2)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316702} :answer-field/id #uuid "5833c098-1ce1-5bf4-b430-b140c6dd9142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77f6137f-8b9e-50aa-8326-ee49fc392f6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-102309" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $6y+9{y}^{″}=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n6{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+9{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the second sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n6{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+9{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the second summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n6{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+9{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[6{a}_{n}+9(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n6{a}_{n}+9(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=-\\frac{2{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=-\\frac{3(n+1)(n+2)}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ab3f10-aca4-5094-98d2-0de9c11fd711" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d423ad7f-d69c-513f-a7e3-daef2fa77942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{2{a}_{n-1}}{3n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316706} {:answer/id #uuid "4b789450-69bf-5553-a729-52977f536449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-6{a}_{n-1}(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316707} {:answer/id #uuid "683fc09b-f4f3-5e57-a1f7-ea87223c616f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316708} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3f57b8-15ca-579a-942b-d51270659a6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-5{a}_{n-1}(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316709} {:answer/id #uuid "5313a7ae-be7b-538f-a191-d586773a9d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3{a}_{n-1}}{2(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5313a7ae-be7b-538f-a191-d586773a9d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3{a}_{n-1}}{2(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316710} :answer-field/id #uuid "23ee027e-b6dd-56fa-a575-375b3c007d48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "17d14283-fdda-5f13-b86f-e9cd3b8d6301" :question/math-academy-id "q-102312" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3xy-2{y}^{′}=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}-2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the second sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}-{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }2(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }2(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}-2{a}_{1}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[3{a}_{n-1}-2(n+1){a}_{n+1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,2(n+1){a}_{n+1}=3{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3{a}_{n-1}}{2(n+1)},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ab3f10-aca4-5094-98d2-0de9c11fd711" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5300a6f7-1edd-5f11-94e0-61fdc5ef6652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{3{a}_{n-1}}{(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316713} {:answer/id #uuid "fef616b0-7436-53be-9a81-8a018e7e6be3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{3(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316714} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b77e40-ff8a-5f6a-815c-fdcf50dcf24f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=3{a}_{n-1}(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316715} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2db70a-8906-5abe-a0c8-dd5e811be64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{(3n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316716} {:answer/id #uuid "35d99852-1c78-5917-b55a-4ae00e1ee5d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-{a}_{n-1}(3n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5300a6f7-1edd-5f11-94e0-61fdc5ef6652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{3{a}_{n-1}}{(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316713} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a7598a7-4351-5eda-86e6-59b96de7b3c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b23fe339-e5a3-520f-aea5-79137303ca56" :question/math-academy-id "q-102317" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $6xy+2{y}^{′}=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the second sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}+{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }2(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }2(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}+2{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n2{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6{a}_{n-1}+2(n+1){a}_{n+1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,2(n+1){a}_{n+1}=-6{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{3{a}_{n-1}}{(n+1)},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d058486e-3e14-5649-815a-7730311c5aec" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For an Exponential Growth or Decay Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7437e0ac-9f00-5b85-ba45-a606c7eda790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{5{a}_{n}}{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316720} {:answer/id #uuid "b6536593-e426-5c91-a402-0587cd017904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{5{a}_{n}}{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316721} {:answer/id #uuid "2fe01dbf-9faa-527e-a232-7319de277085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-5n{a}_{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316722} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c91b12-a221-5a24-99fc-abfbcdb237d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{5(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316723} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3f0981-f75e-5e4b-8b79-61193c4107d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-5{a}_{n}(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7437e0ac-9f00-5b85-ba45-a606c7eda790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{5{a}_{n}}{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316720} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2db850f-1e0c-5d89-89ee-3d979077f544" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b6d4f7db-2063-514e-8996-30801ae185d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-365775" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation ${y}^{′}+5y=0.$ \n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=0,1,2…?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+5{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+5{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+5{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}+5{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n(n+1){a}_{n+1}+5{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{5{a}_{n}}{n+1},\\,n=0,1,2….\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1980ddeb-37bf-5234-8888-f8292e2c9405" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For a Second-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f4749c1-d25d-5b58-acec-6e0f52ff6cb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=(n+1)(n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316727} {:answer/id #uuid "646b1a3c-f711-5e2b-b9f9-0af425c82ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=3(n+1)(2n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316728} {:answer/id #uuid "448c83d1-121b-5f7f-a5d2-1e420a901f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=3(n+1)(n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316729} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3fe36a-68bf-5d0f-bfe9-5cc3c6aeb9ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=3(n+1)(n+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316730} {:answer/id #uuid "8878390e-be7b-5904-ad7b-bed01e2f8369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=3n(n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "448c83d1-121b-5f7f-a5d2-1e420a901f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=3(n+1)(n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316729} :answer-field/id #uuid "93e60e72-c4c2-5a45-988a-50b9506b6a73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "035a71b2-43f1-5331-8a34-23bb98ad68e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-365780" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{″}-y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[3(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-{a}_{n}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n3(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=3(n+1)(n+2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d058486e-3e14-5649-815a-7730311c5aec" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For an Exponential Growth or Decay Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c83a90d-2bcf-536c-be80-ff9343f38354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{4{a}_{n}}{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316734} {:answer/id #uuid "55e9d8b9-5abf-5a47-9cf7-a75f0fd6b62d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=4{a}_{n}(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316735} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d0cc7c-2e0f-5b74-9951-f1b28788e3ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{4(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316736} {:answer/id #uuid "25f05ca5-ec8f-5618-a78f-1a80b88d239a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{4n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316737} {:answer/id #uuid "9c294c2f-f774-5bd7-a2cf-667e60119684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{4(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1d0cc7c-2e0f-5b74-9951-f1b28788e3ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{4(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316736} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4333941-fa0c-576c-b1ba-55e261ceeb98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "951aa831-fbe3-556e-b499-16fb98fc86cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-365781" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $4{y}^{′}-y=0.$ \n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=0,1,2…?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[4(n+1){a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n4(n+1){a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{4(n+1)},\\,n=0,1,2….\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d058486e-3e14-5649-815a-7730311c5aec" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For an Exponential Growth or Decay Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26b527b5-adf6-5f2f-8b8c-efa8c0d64562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-3(n+1){a}_{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316741} {:answer/id #uuid "a26f8da3-ef62-596f-94c2-2519b6a46952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316742} {:answer/id #uuid "b253da26-16a7-5cb8-9363-f458554706b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}+3}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316743} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef98098-9e1e-50d9-a3ac-98b0901d95f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{3{a}_{n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/re